1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 Nc6 3. f4 Bc5 4. Nf3 d6 5. Nc3 ({By transposition, a variation
of the King's Gambit Declined has been reached.} 5. c3 {is equally playable.})
5... Nf6 6. d3 Bg4 7. Na4 Nd7 ({Normal is} 7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qd1 b5 {
with complications.}) 8. Nxc5 Nxc5 9. O-O Ne6 ({Not} 9... Nd4 $2 10. Bxf7+) 10.
h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Qd7 {The best move, strengthening the white squares e6 and f7,
and preparing central horse activity, maybe even castling long. This is really
the first crucial point of the. game - White finds a way to hinder the
co-ordination of the Black pieces by holding the king in the centre.} 12. f5
Nc5 {To play either horse to d4 would be time wasting - White subsequently
hopping back by c3 and opening the centre for the bishop pair against Black's
vulnerable king.} 13. Bg5 {Preventing long castling (13...f6? 14 Qh5+) and
hoping to tempt a weakening ...h6.} Na4 ({An ingenious manoeuvre, but the more
natural} 13... Na5 {is probably better; White now has a little combination to
preserve his advantage.}) 14. Bb5 Nb6 15. a4 {Developing the queen's rook - in
some lines it might get to c3 - but chiefly designed to annul Black's next.} a6
16. a5 axb5 17. axb6 Rc8 18. c3 {Limiting Black's scope - 18...cxb6 is clearly
good for White.} Nb8 {To get counterplay on the c-file.} 19. bxc7 Qxc7 20. Ra8
({Seeking a favourable liquidation. This was another difficult decision since
Black can now castle and apparently escape, from his major difficulties. But
also} 20. f6 {(to keep the king in the centre) devalues the bishop and is
likely to lead to a coincidence of nebulous complications and time-trouble.
But White should win, I suppose.}) 20... f6 21. Be3 O-O 22. Qd1 b4 ({Based on
the variation 23 Qb3+ Qf7 24 Qb4?? Nc6 and Black wins. But} 22... Qf7 {
(contesting the White queen's entry) might be better.}) 23. Qb3+ Qf7 24. Qxf7+
Kxf7 25. Ra7 ({It is far from clear that White has anything in the event of} 
25. cxb4 Nc6 {and ...Nxb4 intending Nxd3.}) 25... bxc3 ({Best, as after} 25...
Rc7 26. Bb6 Rd7 27. c4 $1 Nc6 28. Ra4 {White will eventually win.}) 26. Rxb7+
Kg8 27. bxc3 Rxc3 28. Rc1 Rxc1+ ({If} 28... Rxd3 29. Rcc7 {Black soon gets
mated.}) 29. Bxc1 Rd8 30. Ba3 Na6 {Here Black offered a draw.} 31. Kf2 Nc5 ({
Not} 31... Kf8 $2 32. Rb6 Nc5 33. Rxd6 $1 Rxd6 (33... Nxd3+ 34. Rxd3+) 34. Bxc5
Ke7 35. Ke3 {with a winning kings and pawns ending for White.}) 32. Bxc5 dxc5
33. Ke3 Rc8 ({An extremely interesting and instructive rook and pawn ending
has arisen in which White's winning chances reside in his better pawns 
(Black's isolated c-pawn) which in turn determine his possibilities of long
entry and the passivity of Black's king. On general principles, Black should
seek to generate active play for his rook by} 33... Ra8 {(intending Ra2) when
White has two possible winning tries:} 34. Rb2 $1 (34. Kd2 $2 Ra2+ 35. Kc3 Rxg2
36. Kc4 {This is simply countered by the advance of the Black h-pawn.}) 34...
Ra3 35. Rc2 Ra5 36. Kd2 {roughly as in the game.}) 34. Rb2 $1 {Priority is the
restraint of ...c4 35 Rc2 c3 36 d4 and White attains the ending R+4P v R+3P 
(same wing), a critical question for theory on which the last word has
certainly not been said. In the case, the prognosis for White would be
excellent (notwithstanding Black having achieved the desirable formation f6 v
f5 & f4) because of White's more active pieces; nonetheless there may be a
defence for Black.} g5 ({Black is unable to find a defence to White's
threatened king entry -} 34... Kf7 35. Kd2 Ke7 36. Rb7+ {- and so plays a
king-side diversion, the chief target of which is a wall-in stalemate. This
loses quickly...}) 35. Kd2 Kg7 (35... Rc7 {would be slightly better.}) 36. Rb7+
Kh6 37. Rf7 Rc6 38. Kc3 Ra6 39. Kc4 ({Not} 39. g4 $4 {, when the stalemate can
be realised by perpetual checks of the rook, once the Black c-pawn is captured.
}) 39... Ra2 40. Kd5 {"One of my earliest adventures with the Bishop's Opening.
" (Tim Harding, 2023) Source: bulletin (with Tim Harding's contemporary
annotations)} (40. Kd5 Rxg2 (40... Ra5 41. Rxf6+) (40... Ra6 41. Kxc5) 41. Ke6
{intending Rxf6+, Rf7+, f6.}) 1-0

